// Numerals: comparing numbers written in any script
//
// Ordering (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) follows one rule, and it does not care which
// script wrote the digits:
//
//   · numeric      when both sides are numbers — a string counts as a number
//                  when `#|…|` would convert it, in any of the 69 scripts
//   · lexicographic when both sides are non-numeric text
//   · an error      when a number meets text that is not a number
//
// So `"१०" > "९"` answers the same question as `"10" > "9"`: 10 against 9, not
// the order of their code points. Anything else would make every script but
// ASCII second-class in its own language.

>> "numeric, ASCII digits:" ¶
>> "  \"10\" > \"9\"    " ("10" > "9") ¶
>> "  \"5\"  > 5      " ("5" > 5) ¶

>> "" ¶
>> "numeric, Devanagari digits — same answers:" ¶
>> "  \"१०\" > \"९\"    " ("१०" > "९") ¶
>> "  \"४२\" > 5      " ("४२" > 5) ¶
>> "  \"१.५\" > \"१.४\"  " ("१.५" > "१.४") ¶

>> "" ¶
>> "text stays lexicographic:" ¶
>> "  \"abc\" > \"abd\" " ("abc" > "abd") ¶

>> "" ¶
>> "equality never coerces — a string is not a number:" ¶
>> "  \"5\" == 5     " ("5" == 5) ¶
>> "  \"५\" == 5     " ("५" == 5) ¶
